WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:08.100
When Steve Jobs introduced the iPhone in 2007,&nbsp;
Chinese labor cost about one U.S. Dollar — less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:12.240
than Thailand or the Philippines&nbsp;
and a bargain compared to Malaysia.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:18.180
Fast-forward fifteen years and wages&nbsp;
have increased nearly everywhere.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.080
But China is in a league of its own.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:26.880
By the time the iPhone 14 was released last year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:30.660
the cost of labor had gone&nbsp;
from one to eight dollars.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:37.680
Today, for the price of one hour of&nbsp;
Chinese labor you can buy 4.5 in Mexico.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:44.280
In fact, the only major Latin American&nbsp;
economy with a higher cost is Chile.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:48.480
Not only is “Made in China” no longer cheap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:53.040
globally speaking, you might&nbsp;
even say it’s expensive.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.880
So, what next for the “World’s Factory”?

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:05.400
Sponsored by Nebula, the platform created,&nbsp;
owned, and loved by creators. Sign up for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:10.440
exclusive, early, and bonus content&nbsp;
and to support PolyMatter directly.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:17.700
This is what two decades of cheap&nbsp;
T-Shirts, TVs, and toys looks like.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:22.140
This is Walmart and Amazon and the Dollar Store.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:25.320
On the left are people.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:31.860
You know about the One Child Policy, but you&nbsp;
may not know what immediately preceded it.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:37.800
Mao was convinced that a large population&nbsp;
was the best deterrent against nuclear war.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:42.960
On his final visit to Moscow, the&nbsp;
chairman bragged about the ease with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:45.540
which his country could lose 300 million people.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:51.300
So, although children — also known&nbsp;
as free farm labor — are desirable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:57.120
in any agricultural society, in China,&nbsp;
this incentive was inscribed in law.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:03.180
Points, which could be redeemed for food, were&nbsp;
awarded based on the size of one’s family.

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:07.140
Surprise! The population exploded.

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:13.320
30 years later, this is what China&nbsp;
looked like in the year 2000.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:19.800
Each rectangle represents an age group&nbsp;
— for example, 55 to 59 year-olds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:27.540
Babies are at the bottom and seniors at the top.&nbsp;
The longer the rectangle, the larger that group.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:35.100
Let’s also add some historical context — the&nbsp;
year each age cohort was born — over here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:42.060
These 45-year olds in the year 2000,&nbsp;
for instance, were born in 1955.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:47.400
Now, the difference in size between&nbsp;
these rectangles may seem fairly minor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:54.120
But see this “minor” difference between&nbsp;
25-29 year olds and 20-24 year olds?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:57.900
That represents 25 million people.

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:03.060
As you can see by the large&nbsp;
number of 25-34 year olds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:06.900
Mao’s “strength in numbers”&nbsp;
baby boom was quite effective.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:11.700
And what’s special about 25-34 year olds?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:19.560
Well, they’re what an economist might classify&nbsp;
as young, dumb, and broke. They’ve recently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:25.380
graduated and they’re hungry to start working,&nbsp;
even for low pay and with poor conditions.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:30.300
In other words, if you were trying&nbsp;
to unleash on the world an endless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:33.300
supply of affordable toasters and cheap T-Shirts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:39.120
you could hardly have designed a more perfect&nbsp;
demographic structure than early-2000s China.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:45.060
In summary: your cheaply-made&nbsp;
iPhone 3G was a distant ripple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:48.840
of Mao Zedong’s 1960s-plan to survive nuclear war.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:53.640
The second ingredient to this generation of cheap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:58.020
mass-produced consumerism we&nbsp;
take for granted was migration.

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:02.760
All those baby boomers were&nbsp;
born in rural communes way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:05.700
out in China’s geographic equivalent of Montana.

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:11.280
Reaping the fruits of industrialization&nbsp;
required that they pack their bags&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:14.820
and travel hundreds of miles&nbsp;
East, toward the urban coast.

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:22.680
Between 1990 and 2010, ten million new fresh-faced&nbsp;
migrants made this journey each and every year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:28.380
And at this rate, it’s awfully hard&nbsp;
to imagine ever running out of labor.

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:33.540
But beneath these impressively&nbsp;
large numbers, China was just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:37.380
going through the same demographic&nbsp;
transition as every other country.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:44.760
In the first stage, you have an agricultural&nbsp;
society. Without the aid of tractors or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:50.640
harvesters or sprinklers, there’s only enough food&nbsp;
to survive if everyone spends their time farming.

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:53.160
And, on the farm, remember,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:58.680
children are free labor. Just don’t expect&nbsp;
to live very long without modern medicine.

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:04.260
Lots of births, lots of deaths.&nbsp;
China during the Cultural Revolution.

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:11.700
Next, you start building a few rudimentary&nbsp;
machines — nothing fancy but enough to allow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:18.780
a single farmer to produce rice for, say, one&nbsp;
point one people. Then something magical happens:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:23.520
someone else’s time is freed up to&nbsp;
focus on something like antibiotics.

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:30.900
Mortality goes down, but fertility&nbsp;
stays high. China in the 70s and 80s.

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:37.020
As time goes on, more and more farmers put&nbsp;
down their shovels and move to the city.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:42.600
And in the city, children turn&nbsp;
from valuable assets to expensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:47.100
liabilities. Fertility, therefore, starts to fall.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:54.720
Now, for simplicity, imagine just three&nbsp;
groups of people — kids, adults, and seniors.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:01.920
Because mortality was until recently high, you&nbsp;
don’t yet have a lot of 80 and 90 year-olds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:07.200
Because fertility was previously&nbsp;
high, you now have lots of adults.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:12.540
And because fertility is currently&nbsp;
very low, you don’t have many kids.

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:20.760
This is your country’s golden opportunity — what’s&nbsp;
called a “demographic dividend”. A few short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:26.760
decades where you have lots of working-age adults,&nbsp;
with few kids or seniors to financially support.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:33.480
If you can put them all to work, you can ride&nbsp;
this demographic wave all the way to modernity.

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:36.660
And that’s exactly what China did.

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:42.000
The only difference is that China’s&nbsp;
demographic dividend happened on an extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:49.200
condensed timeline. It ramped up incredibly&nbsp;
quickly and is now collapsing all at once.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:53.460
Before Mao died, here in 1976,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:57.600
he was actively trying to expand the&nbsp;
population as quickly as possible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:03.780
Naturally, 30 years later, all those baby boomers&nbsp;
entered the labor market at the same time.

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:11.460
Immediately after Mao died, here, the One Child&nbsp;
Policy suddenly made high fertility illegal. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:14.100
today, the labor market is suddenly shrinking.

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:20.040
For the last few decades, only diaper&nbsp;
companies have felt this dearth of children.&nbsp;

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:23.760
Economically speaking, kids are pretty useless.

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:30.960
But today, those kids are no longer kids —&nbsp;
and their absence is an absence of labor.

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:37.200
Remember those millions of&nbsp;
extra 25-34 year-olds from 2000?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:42.360
They’re now, on average, 53, and&nbsp;
rounding the corner to retirement.

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:47.640
What was once a demographic&nbsp;
dividend has become a deficit.

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:54.300
Needless to say, China today is the youngest&nbsp;
it will likely ever be in our lifetimes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:57.660
If you’re currently 20 and live to 90,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.660 --> 00:08:02.280
you can plausibly expect to see its&nbsp;
total population decrease by about 40%.

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:08.520
The rate of urbanization, likewise,&nbsp;
can only go as high as 100%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:14.100
China is reaching what economists&nbsp;
call the “Lewis Turning Point”.

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:22.080
The total number of Chinese workers peaked back&nbsp;
in 2015 and has declined every year but one since.

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:28.320
So, where does this leave companies&nbsp;
who rely on this disappearing labor?

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:31.440
In one of three categories.

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:36.900
First, if China is your primary&nbsp;
market, you might be inclined to stay.

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:42.120
If not, the question is: do&nbsp;
you require skilled labor?

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:47.580
If the answer is ‘no’, your&nbsp;
company left China a long time ago.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:52.500
There’s no reason to pay four times&nbsp;
more when you could move to, say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:56.580
Mexico — especially given its&nbsp;
proximity to American consumers.

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:02.280
Just 21% of Nike footwear, for&nbsp;
example, is still made in China.

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:06.960
Finally, by dedu ction, it’s&nbsp;
safe to assume any company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:10.140
that hasn’t left cares about more than just cost.

00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:16.980
It helps that China has 62 million more&nbsp;
20-year olds than Vietnam has people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:24.000
You can’t beat its scale or flexibility.&nbsp;
Apple can hire a small country’s worth of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:27.360
temporary workers right before&nbsp;
the next iPhone is released.

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:36.540
Infrastructure is also important. Roads, ports,&nbsp;
bridges… Chinese cities roll out the red carpet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:41.640
After all, the careers of local bureaucrats&nbsp;
depended on their ability to attract businesses.

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:49.740
Now, these companies have and will diversify,&nbsp;
opening a few factories in Vietnam or India,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:53.880
but they won’t leave China until&nbsp;
they’re dragged kicking and screaming.

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:59.820
We know they can’t easily escape because they&nbsp;
just put up with three years of lockdowns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:01.680
protests, and travel restrictions.

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:08.580
Apple used to reserve 50 business-class tickets&nbsp;
between San Francisco and Shanghai per day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:13.920
You didn’t hear Tim Cook complaining when&nbsp;
those flights disappeared for a thousand days.

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:21.420
Likewise, although China’s labor force is in&nbsp;
decline, its consumer market is still large and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:27.780
relatively untapped. Starbucks, Walmart,&nbsp;
and McDonald’s will continue expanding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:30.720
not shrinking in the country&nbsp;
for the foreseeable future.

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:37.800
That’s what happens to companies.&nbsp;
But what about China, the country?

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:41.760
Previously, the large working-age population&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:45.720
kept the cost of labor down,&nbsp;
leading to increased profit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:51.780
Confident in its continued growth, companies then&nbsp;
used that profit to invest back in the country.

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:58.020
But as the population begins to&nbsp;
decrease, so too does foreign investment.

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:04.740
That puts China in a tough position&nbsp;
— its labor is too expensive to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:09.360
competitive on the low end, but not&nbsp;
skilled enough to occupy the high end.

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:15.480
This is called the “Middle Income Trap”&nbsp;
and the only way out is education.

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:22.500
Greater skills equal higher wages. And&nbsp;
those higher wages are spent on cars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:28.260
phones, and vacations — consumption&nbsp;
that neatly replaces lost investment.

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:32.880
And man oh man does China have education.

00:11:33.900 --> 00:11:41.520
College enrollment tripled between 2000 and 2015.&nbsp;
Performance on the Gaokao — the college entrance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:48.660
exam — is less a score than an identity. And the&nbsp;
after-school tutoring arms-race became so intense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:55.380
that the government was forced to intervene. If&nbsp;
China had a state religion, it might be education.

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:01.080
The problem is not so much a&nbsp;
shortage as an uneven distribution.

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:07.560
A tiny minority are extremely well educated&nbsp;
while the vast majority are left behind.

00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:11.700
The country may churn out bachelor, graduate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:16.620
and professional degrees, but 70%&nbsp;
lack even a high school education.

00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:20.160
What it really needs is something in the middle.

00:12:21.420 --> 00:12:24.060
These days, even, quote, “low-skill” factory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:28.440
work requires skills those without a&nbsp;
high school diploma may be missing.

00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:33.120
Meanwhile, there are only so&nbsp;
many glamorous white-collar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:37.620
tech jobs — the kind China’s college&nbsp;
graduates were told awaited them.

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:43.500
In other words, most of the labor force&nbsp;
either lacks the skills required of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:49.620
mechanic, machine worker, or technician, or is&nbsp;
unwilling to do what they see as “menial work”.

00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:54.600
The result of this labor&nbsp;
mismatch is mass unemployment.

00:12:55.740 --> 00:12:59.580
Last year, the youth unemployment&nbsp;
rate reached nearly 20%.

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:07.500
Now, China has been trying to tackle&nbsp;
these challenges since at least 2007,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.500 --> 00:13:12.000
when Wen Jiabo announced the shift&nbsp;
to a consumption-driven economy.

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:19.140
But little progress has been made since.&nbsp;
Chinese household consumption as a share of GDP,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.140 --> 00:13:23.160
for instance, remains nearly&nbsp;
half that of the U.S. or U.K.

00:13:24.540 --> 00:13:29.160
For years, the “rise of China” was&nbsp;
considered a near inevitability.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:33.900
Magazine covers depicted giant&nbsp;
dragons smothering the globe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.500 --> 00:13:39.780
Book covers portrayed red, white, and blue&nbsp;
eagles being trampled by giant, red pandas.

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:45.300
Increasingly, however, these predictions&nbsp;
resemble the anxious, motivated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:50.340
and sometimes xenophobic portrayals&nbsp;
of the Japanese economy in the 1980s.

00:13:51.480 --> 00:13:56.940
Beneath these narratives was an implicit&nbsp;
assumption: that China would continue on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.940 --> 00:14:03.420
its current trajectory. That the “demographic&nbsp;
golden age” would last forever. That it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.420 --> 00:14:07.740
somehow be miraculously unimpeded by the&nbsp;
challenges faced by every other country.

00:14:09.720 --> 00:14:14.340
But the simple fact is, these&nbsp;
are really hard problems.

00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:21.840
Investing in vocational schools is not enough — it&nbsp;
also must reverse the stigma which surrounds them.

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:25.380
Neither is convincing couples to simply have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.380 --> 00:14:29.400
more children — it has to make&nbsp;
things child care affordable.

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:36.840
And it can’t just induce consumption — it has to&nbsp;
address the root cause — unaffordable housing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.840 --> 00:14:39.060
which swallows up people’s savings.

00:14:39.720 --> 00:14:45.420
It’s no wonder that out of 101&nbsp;
countries between 1960 and 2012,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.420 --> 00:14:49.380
only a dozen or so escaped the Middle Income Trap.

00:14:50.580 --> 00:14:57.780
A more realistic preview of China’s future may lie&nbsp;
in the actual fate of Japan — still a large and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.780 --> 00:15:03.600
influential economy but severely constrained by&nbsp;
fundamental realities like a declining birth rate.

00:15:04.860 --> 00:15:09.900
In the meantime, one of the most fascinating&nbsp;
products of late-globalization are China’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:13.200
highly-specialized factory towns. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:16.680
the one town that produced most of&nbsp;
the world’s Christmas decorations.

00:15:17.700 --> 00:15:23.880
Another one, Baotou, at one point produced 60%&nbsp;
of the world’s rare earth metals and we explore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.880 --> 00:15:28.380
its rise in this episode of “China,&nbsp;
Actually”, my Nebula Original series.

00:15:28.380 --> 00:15:35.400
You might be wondering why Nebula? The&nbsp;
answer is simple: because it’s ours.

00:15:36.120 --> 00:15:39.480
Running a YouTube channel is&nbsp;
like renting a space in a mall.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.080 --> 00:15:46.500
Sure, the mall brings us foot traffic and for that&nbsp;
we’re grateful. But we’re also at its mercy. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.500 --> 00:15:53.340
our interests aren’t always aligned. After all,&nbsp;
